A1. The miss of the ball. ~ FUMBLE
A7. To excite agreeable sensations. ~ PLEASE
A12. What Chinese fight over. ~ ASIA
A13. This is a wash-away. ~ RINSE
A16. Unauthorized state of being gone (init.). ~ AWOL
A17. You (pardon my French). ~ TU
A18. This poor fish is often at sea. ~ SHAD
A20. Just a dear little girl in Scotland. ~ LASS
A22. This never arrives until midnight. ~ AM
A23. In addition. ~ ALSO
A25. "I" trouble. ~ EGO
A27. Where is Pikes Peak? ~ ATOP
A29. A signal flashed from the car ahead. ~ SLO
A30. When Doc cuts up he needs this. ~ SCALPEL
A33. A way to change color without blushing. ~ DYE
A35. An insect that is leaps ahead of all the rest. ~ FLEA
A37. This old boy was always fiddling around. ~ NERO
A39. This is always up against it (prefix). ~ ANTI
A41. This suit is too hot for most people. ~ LIBEL
A44. This is always dry. ~ ARID
A46. What were the three men in a boat? ~ TRIO
A47. What George probably did when he said he couldn't tell a lie. ~ LIED
A48. A club of glad boys. ~ GLEE
A50. Parts of a ship it's hard to sleep in. ~ BUNKS
A52. This kind of Scotch is cold. ~ ICED
A55. Never false. ~ TRUE
A57. What the plumber went home for. ~ TOOL
A59. Vegetable container. ~ SAC
A61. That excessively virtuous feeling. ~ PRUDISH
A64. A high flyer. ~ ACE
A66. Chase away. ~ SHOO
A68. This catches many a poor fish. ~ NET
A69. The "eats" are on this. ~ MENU
A71. Exclamation of surprise. ~ OH
A72. A small two-winged fly. ~ GNAT
A74. This is easy to take. ~ EASE
A76. A wee little State in the Union (init.). ~ RI
A78. This is always behind. ~ REAR
A80. Found in the best houses. ~ ROOMS
A82. This comes after all the others. ~ LAST
A84. To set a value on. ~ ESTEEM
A85. You'd love to get these.' ~ KISSES

D1. Those who live off the fat of the land. ~ FAT
D2. This is customary. ~ USUAL
D3. A note in the musical scale. ~ MI
D4. A sea fish. ~ BASS
D5. A long, long time. ~ ERA
D6. A good place to put your hat on a rainy day. ~ ON
D7. A plant with papilionaceous flowers. ~ PEA
D8. A direction. ~ EAST
D9. American women (init.). ~ AW
D10. The faces that are easiest to shave. ~ SOAPY
D11. A tree. ~ ELM
D14. Hard to get—and should be executed. ~ IDEA
D15. To spill. ~ SLOP
D19. They'd fall if it weren't for their supporters. ~ HOSE
D21. What good salesmen should think about. ~ SALE
D24. This is always upstairs. ~ LOFT
D26. Smooth; fluent. ~ GLIB
D28. This has no nose—but smells. ~ ODOR
D29. This is all wet; but very deep. ~ SEA
D31. Big guns have lots of this. ~ CALIBER
D32. What Jack does to see the world. ~ ENLISTS
D34. This comes at the finish. ~ END
D36. A French standard measure. ~ LITER
D38. This takes the air. ~ RADIO
D40. This amounts to nothing. ~ NIL
D42. This is easy to get but hard to collect. ~ IOU
D43. A member of the deer family. ~ ELK
D45. With this you're sure to be angry. ~ IRE
D48. Man's nickname. ~ GUS
D49. Only artists should attempt to do this. ~ ETCH
D51. There's nothing on this. ~ NUDE
D53. Scotch used to come in this. ~ CLAN
D54. What the Old Year did. ~ DIE
D56. Where equestrians should stay. ~ UPON
D58. Electrical resistance units. ~ OHMS
D60. The end of all good cigars. ~ ASHES
D62. To (Biblical). ~ UNTO
D63. Always found in the papers. ~ ITEM
